1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Early-stage companies are
uncertain by nature.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:08,136
A startup can run out of cash, fail
to find customers, lose a key

3
00:00:08,136 --> 00:00:12,700
supplier, face litigation, or be
unable to raise its next round.

4
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:16,422
If it closes, creditors and other
security holders may have

5
00:00:16,422 --> 00:00:20,145
priority over you, and there may
be nothing left for common

6
00:00:20,145 --> 00:00:22,038
shareholders or a SAFE holder.

7
00:00:22,038 --> 00:00:26,309
That means losing the full amount
is possible, not just seeing the

8
00:00:26,309 --> 00:00:27,862
number temporarily fall.

9
00:00:27,862 --> 00:00:32,202
A startup can also keep operating
while your particular security

10
00:00:32,202 --> 00:00:36,000
never converts, pays no
distribution, or finds no buyer.

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,238
Review risks that are specific to

12
00:00:38,238 --> 00:00:40,950
the company, not only a generic
warning.

13
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:44,606
Ask what must go right for the
plan to work, how much cash is

14
00:00:44,606 --> 00:00:48,562
available, and what happens if the
raise is smaller than expected.

15
00:00:48,562 --> 00:00:52,126
Only consider money you can
afford to lose without putting

16
00:00:52,126 --> 00:00:53,600
essential needs at risk.

17
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:57,282
Diversifying can reduce
dependence on one company, but

18
00:00:57,282 --> 00:01:01,032
it cannot eliminate startup risk or
guarantee a return.

